{"qid":"Q664","names":["New Zealand"],"title":"New Zealand","endonym":{"native":"Aotearoa","lang":"mi"},"capsule":"New Zealand (Aotearoa) achieved full independence as a constitutional monarchy within the Commonwealth through the New Zealand Independence Act 1947, which brought the Statute of Westminster into full domestic effect. The country is a parliamentary democracy of approximately five million people occupying two main islands in the southwestern Pacific, with a significant Māori minority whose Treaty of Waitangi (1840) relationship with the Crown became an increasingly central element of constitutional and social policy across the late twentieth century. New Zealand pioneered women's suffrage in 1893, established a comprehensive welfare state by the 1930s, and subsequently undertook dramatic market liberalization after 1984. It maintains a permanent presence in Antarctica (the Ross Dependency) and administers the Cook Islands, Niue, and Tokelau.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Territorial extent is precisely delimited by international treaty; internal jurisdictional questions around Māori land do not affect the geopolitical polygon.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: New Zealand","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/New-Zealand"},{"label":"Wikipedia: New Zealand","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Zealand"},{"label":"Keith Sinclair, A History of New Zealand (Penguin, 1959, rev. 1980)","url":"https://archive.org/details/historyofnewzeal00sinc"}],"britannica":"place/New-Zealand","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"New Zealand","rulers":[{"qid":"Q1400226","name":"Peter Fraser","start